DAY_DATA = {'day': 153, 'part1_intro': "Day 153 — EHR Healthcare — defend the design — uses the roadmap's case or exam scope as the input. Each topic names the decision boundary, evidence owner and business effect; no product choice or score is treated as a fact without supporting evidence.", 'part2_intro': 'Trace the full reasoning path: supplied fact or requirement, candidate design or study rule, acceptance evidence, failure signal, owner and condition that would change the choice. The topology is a teaching model, not evidence of a live system or exam result.', 'part3_intro': 'These deep-dive cases preserve the original case or exam reasoning while separating supplied facts from architectural inference. The incident diagrams are synthetic exercises, not observed production events.', 'part4_intro': "The labs retain each day's original decision work, expanded into the eight execution stages used on Day 142. Complete them with prior artifacts and synthetic inputs unless an earlier roadmap page explicitly authorizes a cloud test.", 'exit_summary': 'A one-page design comparison, ADR, cost assumptions and risk list; update the regulated-workload and migration capstones with transferable lessons.', 'arch_table_html': '\n<table><caption>Day-specific architecture and evidence path</caption><thead><tr><th>Topic boundary</th><th>Decision rule</th><th>Acceptance evidence</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Compare the candidate architecture against the EHR fact register</td><td>Separate supplied fact, requirement and proposed control.</td><td>One artifact, one rejected alternative and one changed-constraint result.</td><td>Local/tabletop evidence cannot prove production behavior or exam performance.</td></tr></tbody></table>', 'arch_diagram': {'type': 'topology', 'title': 'Day 153: EHR Healthcare — defend the design — evidence and decision path', 'desc': 'Three-tier architecture path for EHR Healthcare — defend the design, showing case or exam inputs, requirements and trade-off evidence, then acceptance and ownership controls.', 'caption': 'Scope: a synthetic evidence path for Day 153 covering Compare the candidate architecture against. It shows reasoning boundaries and verification ownership; it does not prove a production deployment, exam result, price, quota, eligibility or customer outcome.', 'width': 1120, 'height': 690, 'layers': [{'name': 'TIER 1 · INPUTS / DEMAND', 'desc': 'case facts, prompts and constraints', 'x': 10, 'y': 55, 'w': 1100, 'h': 92, 'fill': '#0c2033', 'title_color': '#38bdf8', 'opacity': 0.72}, {'name': 'TIER 2 · EVIDENCE / REASONING', 'desc': 'requirements, alternatives and failure checks', 'x': 10, 'y': 185, 'w': 1100, 'h': 210, 'fill': '#241808', 'title_color': '#f59e0b', 'opacity': 0.64}, {'name': 'TIER 3 · GOVERNANCE / ACCEPTANCE', 'desc': 'owners, acceptance and revisit triggers', 'x': 10, 'y': 435, 'w': 1100, 'h': 115, 'fill': '#062316', 'title_color': '#22c55e', 'opacity': 0.68}], 'components': [{'name': 'Case or prompt', 'detail': 'Day 153 supplied scope', 'x': 60, 'y': 78, 'w': 250, 'h': 56, 'stroke': '#38bdf8', 'fill': '#102a42'}, {'name': 'Prior artifacts', 'detail': 'facts + decisions + errors', 'x': 435, 'y': 78, 'w': 250, 'h': 56, 'stroke': '#38bdf8', 'fill': '#102a42'}, {'name': 'Changed constraint', 'detail': 'cost / risk / scope', 'x': 810, 'y': 78, 'w': 250, 'h': 56, 'stroke': '#38bdf8', 'fill': '#102a42'}, {'name': 'Requirements map', 'detail': 'fact to acceptance', 'x': 60, 'y': 220, 'w': 250, 'h': 105, 'stroke': '#f59e0b', 'fill': '#3b2508'}, {'name': 'Option matrix', 'detail': 'choice + alternative', 'x': 435, 'y': 220, 'w': 250, 'h': 105, 'stroke': '#f59e0b', 'fill': '#3b2508'}, {'name': 'Failure rehearsal', 'detail': 'signal + safe response', 'x': 810, 'y': 220, 'w': 250, 'h': 105, 'stroke': '#f59e0b', 'fill': '#3b2508'}, {'name': 'Evidence owner', 'detail': 'source + artifact', 'x': 245, 'y': 455, 'w': 250, 'h': 70, 'stroke': '#22c55e', 'fill': '#064e3b'}, {'name': 'Acceptance gate', 'detail': 'observed / predicted', 'x': 625, 'y': 455, 'w': 250, 'h': 70, 'stroke': '#22c55e', 'fill': '#064e3b'}], 'flows': [{'x1': 185, 'y1': 134, 'x2': 185, 'y2': 220, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'extract'}, {'x1': 560, 'y1': 134, 'x2': 560, 'y2': 220, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'trace'}, {'x1': 935, 'y1': 134, 'x2': 935, 'y2': 220, 'type': 'warn', 'label': 'challenge'}, {'x1': 310, 'y1': 272, 'x2': 435, 'y2': 272, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'compare'}, {'x1': 685, 'y1': 272, 'x2': 810, 'y2': 272, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'rehearse'}, {'x1': 935, 'y1': 325, 'x2': 750, 'y2': 455, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'accept'}, {'x1': 560, 'y1': 325, 'x2': 370, 'y2': 455, 'type': 'warn', 'label': 'evidence'}, {'x1': 495, 'y1': 490, 'x2': 625, 'y2': 490, 'type': 'ok', 'label': 'review'}], 'boundaries': [{'x': 42, 'y': 205, 'w': 1036, 'h': 140, 'color': '#f59e0b', 'label': 'EVIDENCE CHECK'}, {'x': 220, 'y': 442, 'w': 680, 'h': 94, 'color': '#22c55e', 'label': 'DECISION OWNER'}], 'probes': [{'cx': 185, 'cy': 106, 'label': 'PROBE 1 · input classified', 'badge': 'P1', 'color': '#38bdf8'}, {'cx': 560, 'cy': 272, 'label': 'PROBE 2 · trade-off recorded', 'badge': 'P2', 'color': '#f59e0b'}, {'cx': 750, 'cy': 490, 'label': 'PROBE 3 · acceptance result', 'badge': 'P3', 'color': '#22c55e'}]}, 'topics': [{'key': 'topic-01', 'title': 'Compare the candidate architecture against the EHR fact register', 'overview': 'Today is a design defense, not a product memorization exercise. Start with the EHR case’s stated business, security, migration and recovery constraints. Compare feasible designs at the boundary where a requirement changes the choice: data consistency, integration shape, recovery target, operator skills, auditability or cost. Keep three labels visible in every row: supplied fact , architectural inference and question requiring confirmation . A polished diagram without that distinction is not defensible evidence.', 'preview': 'Symptom: A review board receives a diagram that places the EHR workload on a preferred database and runtime, but the proposal does not show the source dependencies, change-capture path, audit owner, restore test or rollback trigger. Effect: The compare the candidate architecture against the ehr fact register decision remains unverified.', 'technical': '\n<p>The decision begins with a requirements-to-boundary map, not a database name. For every EHR requirement, write the owner, data boundary, failure consequence, acceptance signal and the fact that would change the choice. The official Professional Cloud Architect guide frames case questions around business requirements, migration planning, data movement, security, recovery, cost and trade-offs; it does not prescribe one implementation for every case.</p>\n<table><caption>EHR design-defense matrix</caption><thead><tr><th>Decision boundary</th><th>Candidate A</th><th>Candidate B</th><th>Evidence that decides</th><th>Risk if assumed</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Transactional data</td><td>Managed relational service with compatibility-first migration</td><td>Distributed relational service for a changed scale or availability requirement</td><td>Schema features, write ownership, measured migration throughput, stated recovery target</td><td>Choosing distributed storage can add cost and operational complexity without a case requirement.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Application runtime</td><td>Managed Kubernetes for an existing containerized estate</td><td>Managed compute or serverless runtime for a smaller, independently deployable path</td><td>Container dependency, startup profile, network reachability, team skills and deployment evidence</td><td>Assuming Kubernetes is automatically simpler transfers control-plane and platform work to the team.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hybrid connectivity</td><td>Private hybrid path with explicit routing, DNS and firewall ownership</td><td>Staged data transfer with a temporary, tightly bounded integration path</td><td>Data-movement volume, cutover window, dependency inventory, return-path test</td><td>A green connectivity test may cover only a probe, not the full migration path.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Protection and audit</td><td>Least-privilege identities, customer-managed keys where required, and centralized audit evidence</td><td>Platform defaults plus compensating controls where the case permits them</td><td>Access matrix, key ownership, log retention, sensitive-data boundary and review owner</td><td>Encryption at rest alone does not prove authorization, key custody or usable audit evidence.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Recovery</td><td>Backup/restore with a rehearsed rollback point</td><td>Replicated or failover design with explicit write and reconciliation rules</td><td>Declared RTO/RPO, restore timing, consistency check and failback owner</td><td>A topology diagram does not prove that recovery meets the target.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>For the migration, separate <em>copy</em>, <em>change capture</em>, <em>validation</em>, <em>cutover</em> and <em>rollback</em>. The acceptance invariant is not “the command completed”; it is that the required records, identities, permissions and business operations reconcile after the transition. If an event can be replayed, retain a durable idempotency key and prove that one order still produces one fulfillment.</p>\n<p>For security, trace a real actor through authentication, authorization, network reachability, data access and audit recording. IAM roles, service identities, key access, firewall policy, private API paths and log sinks have different owners. For recovery, state what happens to writes during a partition, what data is authoritative, and who is allowed to declare rollback. Mark any answer that depends on current product limits or pricing for verification before implementation.</p>\n<div class="callout"><strong>Worked defense pattern</strong><p>“I prefer Candidate A because the supplied case requires compatibility with an existing relational workload and the fact register shows no cross-region write requirement. Candidate B becomes preferable if the owner confirms a higher availability target or a scale boundary that Candidate A cannot meet. Before approval I need measured migration throughput, restore evidence, the identity matrix and a dated price estimate.” This is stronger than naming a service without a decision rule.</p></div>\n<div class="further"><h4>Further study</h4><p><a href="../sources.html#topic-046">Topic 046 source section</a> links to the current Professional Cloud Architect exam guide. Use the <a href="https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_architect_exam_guide_english.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">official exam guide</a> for the case-study context and architecture objectives; page content and product terminology can change.</p></div>\n<p><a href="../sources.html#topic-046">Topic 046 source section</a>; current publisher material was checked 2026-09-30. Separate supplied facts, local observations and proposed controls.</p>', 'questions': ['Which supplied fact, requirement or artifact controls the choice?', 'Which credible alternative is rejected, and what evidence would make it preferable?', 'Which failure signal reveals the first broken boundary?', 'What is observed, supplied, predicted or still unknown?'], 'reference': 'https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_architect_exam_guide_english.pdf', 'reference_label': 'Official Topic 46 source (checked 2026-09-30)', 'scenario': {'scenario': 'A review board receives a diagram that places the EHR workload on a preferred database and runtime, but the proposal does not show the source dependencies, change-capture path, audit owner, restore test or rollback trigger. Reviewers cannot tell which statements came from the case and which were invented by the designer. Approval would turn unknown migration and regulated-data risks into an outage or an unprovable compliance claim.', 'impact': '', 'constraints': 'Preserve the case’s supplied facts; do not add patient data to the lab; keep the design within the team’s demonstrated skills; and do not claim a live RTO, RPO, price, regional property or service limit without evidence.', 'facts': 'The case statement and artifact are teaching inputs; no production incident or learner result is claimed.', 'inference': 'The design optimized for a product label before converting the case into acceptance tests. It also combined availability, backup, replication and audit into one vague “secure and highly available” requirement. That hides the first failing boundary: a missing dependency, an untested restore, an unauthorized service identity or an unreconciled write.', 'root': 'The design optimized for a product label before converting the case into acceptance tests. It also combined availability, backup, replication and audit into one vague “secure and highly available” requirement. That hides the first failing boundary: a missing dependency, an untested restore, an unauthorized service identity or an unreconciled write.', 'fix': 'Rebuild the proposal as two alternatives. For each, document data ownership, migration sequence, connectivity, access path, key/log ownership, recovery behavior, cost variables, operator work and a rejected option. Select only after the fact register and one changed-constraint test produce a stable choice. The decision must include a pre-cutover acceptance checklist, a rollback authority and a reconciliation result.', 'verify': 'Run a tabletop failure in which the migration is late, the destination is unavailable, or a duplicate event is replayed. Record the first changed signal and the safe stop point. A tabletop result predicts behavior; it does not prove a deployed EHR service, regulatory approval or customer acceptance.', 'residual': 'Run a tabletop failure in which the migration is late, the destination is unavailable, or a duplicate event is replayed. Record the first changed signal and the safe stop point. A tabletop result predicts behavior; it does not prove a deployed EHR service, regulatory approval or customer acceptance.', 'diagram': ('supplied trigger', 'missing or untested control', 'business and operating impact', 'bounded defensive control', 'verified evidence artifact')}, 'lab': {'name': 'Compare the candidate architecture against the EHR fact register', 'goal': 'Produce a source-linked design defense from the Day 152 fact register, with one selected option, one rejected option, explicit assumptions and a bounded failure rehearsal.', 'expected': 'Expected state A one-page comparison and ADR with a selected design, rejected alternative, owner, evidence classification, changed-constraint result, rollback trigger and unresolved questions. Artifact acceptance Accept only when another architect can reproduce the decision from the fact register, see why the alternative was rejected, and identify who owns the next verification.', 'mode': 'local/tabletop with synthetic data; no cloud access or spend.', 'prereq': 'Day 152 fact register and unresolved-question list.', 'preflight': 'Use `day-153-ehr-design-defense.md` and record the source, access date, prior artifact, owner and synthetic-data boundary.', 'steps': ['**Stage 1 — Preflight and validate assumptions/environment.** Create `day-153-ehr-design-defense.md`. Record Day 153, the prior artifact, the source `https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_architect_exam_guide_english.pdf`, the access date, a named owner and the statement that the exercise uses synthetic or tabletop evidence only.', '**Stage 2 — Prepare the target, inputs, or backing resources.** Preflight. Create a note named day-153-ehr-design-defense.md . Record the date, prior artifact, lab mode, decision owner and “synthetic case data only.” Copy the source URL into the note.', '**Stage 3 — Author the plan, configuration, or analysis.** Freeze the input. Copy the EHR facts from Day 152 into a table with columns claim | source/page or artifact | supplied/inferred/question | owner . Do not fill missing facts with assumptions.', '**Stage 4 — Execute or simulate the planned change.** Draft alternatives. Create Candidate A and Candidate B. For each, fill data | runtime | connectivity | identity | encryption/key | audit | migration | recovery | cost driver | operator skill . Include a credible rejected alternative and the condition that would make it preferable.', '**Stage 5 — Inspect expected state and verify outcomes.** Define acceptance. Add one check for schema/data reconciliation, one for authorization and audit evidence, one for restore/failback, and one for the invariant one order, one fulfillment where the case touches an order path.', '**Stage 6 — Rehearse a bounded failure, edge case, or decision challenge.** Inject a changed constraint. Choose one: the cutover window is shorter, the destination region is unavailable, the source emits late changes, or the recovery target becomes stricter. Record the first boundary that changes and whether the selected design survives.', '**Stage 7 — Diagnose evidence and record remediation/decision.** Defend and record. Write a five-sentence recommendation: choice, evidence, rejected option, trigger to revisit and residual risk. Mark every result as observed locally, supplied by the case, or predicted in the tabletop.', '**Stage 8 — Clean up or close out the exercise.** Close out. Save the note and link it from the daily artifact. Update the regulated-workload and migration capstone notes with the reusable decision rule; remove disposable copies and record what still needs authorized testing or customer confirmation.'], 'verification': 'Expected state A one-page comparison and ADR with a selected design, rejected alternative, owner, evidence classification, changed-constraint result, rollback trigger and unresolved questions. Artifact acceptance Accept only when another architect can reproduce the decision from the fact register, see why the alternative was rejected, and identify who owns the next verification.', 'accept': 'Expected state A one-page comparison and ADR with a selected design, rejected alternative, owner, evidence classification, changed-constraint result, rollback trigger and unresolved questions. Artifact acceptance Accept only when another architect can reproduce the decision from the fact register, see why the alternative was rejected, and identify who owns the next verification.', 'trouble': 'Troubleshooting If both options seem equally good, identify the missing requirement or measurement. If the diagram names a service but not an acceptance signal, replace the name with a testable boundary. Never convert a predicted result into a production claim.', 'cleanup': 'Keep the evidence artifact and remove only disposable copies. No cloud resource, credential, payment detail or protected exam content belongs in this exercise.', 'file': 'day-153-ehr-design-defense.md'}}]}
